    "case_id": "613",
    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old White woman with epilepsy and osteoporosis presented with a 10-day history of worsening, intermittent epigastric pain, distention, and constipation. Pain was nonradiating and cramping. She reported fever, chills, and anorexia. No recent illness, weight loss, dysphagia, vomiting, change in stool caliber, melena, or hematochezia. No gastrointestinal medical or surgical history. Family history included ulcerative colitis in her mother. Prior endoscopic work-up included two screening colonoscopies (as recent as five months prior), but no upper endoscopy.\n\nPatient was afebrile and hemodynamically stable. Physical examination revealed generalized abdominal tenderness with mild distention.\n\n- Laboratory tests:\n  * Leukocyte count: 11.4 K/CUMM\n  * Liver function tests: normal\n  * Lipase: 10 U/L (upper limit of normal, 81 U/L)\n\n- Imaging studies:\n  * Computed Tomography with contrast:\n    Title: Initial CT abdomen and pelvis\n    Description: Revealed duodenal diverticula, wall thickening with mesenteric edema approximately 25 cm beyond the ligament of Treitz, and mesenteric lymphadenopathy with luminal patency\n\n  * Follow-up CT scan (one month later):\n    Title: Follow-up CT abdomen and pelvis\n    Description: Showed resolution of inflammatory changes\n\n- Endoscopic studies:\n  * Capsule endoscopy performed but images of the diverticula were not captured",
